VT_TALK("sql/sql-security", {
 "injection": "SQL injection is one of the oldest and most damaging security flaws on the web. It happens when a program builds SQL by gluing the user's typed text into the query. If someone types a quote, they can close the string early and add their own SQL. The classic example types a quote, then OR one equals one, then two dashes. The OR makes every row match, and the dashes turn the PIN check into a comment, so the login lets them in as anyone. In the animation, type normal names and the attack text, and watch what the database really receives. We show this only so you can recognise and prevent it. Think of a form where someone writes extra instructions in the name box.",
 "params": "The reliable fix is a parameterized query, also called a prepared statement. You write the SQL with question marks or numbered placeholders where the values go, and the values are sent separately. The database reads the SQL first and only then plugs in the values, so a value can never turn into code. The attack text becomes a strange name that matches nobody. Every language has this, Python, JavaScript, Java and the rest. Table and column names cannot be parameters, so choose them from a fixed list in your code. Think of a bank form with separate boxes, where writing in the amount box can never change the account number.",
 "access": "Server databases have accounts, and each account should get only the rights it needs, which is called the principle of least privilege. GRANT gives a right, such as SELECT on a table, and REVOKE takes it away. Roles group rights so you grant them once to many people. The web app can read and write its own tables but never drop them, and the reporting account can only read. A view that leaves out personal columns, granted instead of the table, is a simple privacy shield. SQLite has no accounts, so it relies on file permissions. Think of an office where each key card opens only the rooms that person needs.",
 "rls": "Sometimes many users share one table but each may see only their own rows, like customers and their orders. Row level security makes the database add that filter to every query automatically. PostgreSQL does this with a policy, and in SQLite a view that reads the logged in customer from a session table gives the same effect. Personal data needs more care. Never store passwords, store a slow salted hash made in the app. Mask emails and card numbers on screens. Encrypt data on disk and on the network, keep backups you have actually restored, and log who changed what. Think of a hotel where every key opens only its own room.",
 "levels": "Here is the ladder. For a beginner, never glue typed text into SQL, give accounts only what they need, and protect personal data. At college level, use parameters everywhere, roles and grants, views and row level security, hashed passwords and backups. At masters level, plan defence in depth, and watch for second order injection, where stored text is later glued into SQL. At research level, differential privacy adds careful noise so totals reveal little about any one person. Think of a castle with a moat, walls and guards, so one broken defence is not the end."
});
